\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 Ad Rank\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Ad Rank is a value that Google uses to determine your ad position and whether your ads will show at all. It's calculated using several factors, but for simplicity, we'll focus on two main components:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Your bid amount\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Your Quality Score\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 The basic formula for Ad Rank is:\
Ad Rank = Max Bid \'d7 Quality Score\
In our example, we have three advertisers:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Advertiser A: Bid $2.00, Quality Score 6 Ad Rank = $2.00 \'d7 6 = 12\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Advertiser B: Bid $4.00, Quality Score 3 Ad Rank = $4.00 \'d7 3 = 12\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Advertiser C: Bid $3.00, Quality Score 8 Ad Rank = $3.00 \'d7 8 = 24\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 As you can see, despite Advertiser B having the highest bid at $4.00, they don't win the top position. Here's why:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Advertiser C wins the top position with the highest Ad Rank of 24. They achieved this with a moderate bid ($3.00) but an excellent Quality Score (8).\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Advertiser A takes the second position. Although they have the same Ad Rank as Advertiser B (12), Google typically favors the ad with the higher Quality Score when Ad Ranks are equal.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Advertiser B, despite having the highest bid, ends up in the third position due to their low Quality Score.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 This example illustrates a key principle of Google Ads: a high-quality ad with a lower bid can outperform a low-quality ad with a higher bid. This system encourages advertisers to focus not just on bidding high, but also on creating relevant, high-quality ads and landing pages.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

