\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Ad Positions: Based on the Ad Ranks, the positions are:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Note: When Ad Ranks are tied, the advertiser with the higher Quality Score gets the better position. - Advertiser C: 1st (Ad Rank 24) - Advertiser A: 2nd (Ad Rank 12) - Advertiser B: 3rd (Ad Rank 12) 2. Actual CPC Calculation for C (1st position): Actual CPC = (Ad Rank of the advertiser below / Quality Score of C) + $0.01 = (12 / 8) + $0.01 = $1.51 So, even though Advertiser C bid $3.00, they only pay $1.51 per click. 3. Actual CPC Calculation for A (2nd position): Actual CPC = (Ad Rank of the advertiser below / Quality Score of A) + $0.01 = (12 / 6) + $0.01 = $2.01 In this case, Advertiser A ends up paying $0.01 more than their max bid. This can happen when the Ad Rank of the advertiser below is equal to or higher than your own.\
This example illustrates several important points:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Higher Quality Score can lead to better ad position even with a lower bid: Advertiser C won the top position with a $3.00 bid, beating Advertiser B's $4.00 bid, thanks to a much higher Quality Score.\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 You can pay significantly less than your maximum bid: Advertiser C is paying only $1.51, despite bidding $3.00.\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 In some cases, you might pay your full bid (or slightly more): Advertiser A is paying $2.01, which is $0.01 more than their $2.00 bid. This happens because the advertiser below them (B) has the same Ad Rank.\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Tied Ad Ranks are broken by Quality Score: Although A and B have the same Ad Rank, A gets the higher position due to better Quality Score.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

